\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $N=pq$ be a semiprime and let $s=p+q$ be its \emph{trace}. Fermat-style
factoring scans candidate traces; the \emph{trace-set filter} is the free
residue-local consistency test that accepts a candidate modulo a prime $m\nmid N$
precisely when its residue lies in the \emph{trace set}
$T_m(N)=\{x+Nx^{-1}: x\in(\Zm{m})^{\times}\}$.
We give the complete exact theory of this filter over an arbitrary finite field and
determine, unconditionally, how much it can contribute to a search for $s$ inside an
externally supplied hint interval $[s_0-E,\,s_0+E]$.
We prove: \emph{(i)} exactness, so the filter has zero false negatives at every modulus
simultaneously; \emph{(ii)} the exact size law
$2\card{T(N)}=(\card{K}-1)+\#\{x:x^2=N\}$ over a finite field $K$, which over $\Zm{m}$
reads $2\card{T_m(N)}=m+\chi_m(N)$ with $\chi_m$ the Legendre symbol, so a wrong
candidate survives one prime with probability exactly $\tfrac12(1+\chi_m(N)/m)$;
\emph{(iii)} minimality --- every filter that never rejects the truth contains the whole
trace set, hence retains at least $(m-1)/2$ residues, and this persists for filters that
couple all primes arbitrarily modulo their product; \emph{(iv)} an exact global census:
the filters are Chinese-remainder-independent and positionally blind, so any window of
$M=\prod_i m_i$ consecutive candidates contains exactly
$2^{-\omega}\prod_i(m_i+\chi_{m_i}(N))$ survivors wherever the window sits, and the count
is exactly linear in the width; \emph{(v)} a hard isolation barrier: if the filters leave
at most one candidate in a window of width $W$, then $W<\prod_i m_i$, so isolating a
$k$-bit hint demands $\Omega(k/\log k)$ primes and $2^{\Omega(k/\log k)}$ sieve work.
We also show the factor-residue filter is entirely empty, and that the trace scan is
Fermat's difference-of-squares method in disguise: over $\Z$, $s$ is the trace of a
factorization of $N$ if and only if $s^2-4N$ is a perfect square, with factors
$(s\mp d)/2$. A controlled experiment over $400$ semiprimes with $24$-bit factors
reproduces every prediction to three decimals, including the honest cost accounting:
discriminant tests saved by the filter are traded one-for-one against membership tests
spent applying it.
\end{abstract}

