\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}
Fix a semiprime $N=pq$ with $p\neq q$ prime. Its \emph{trace} is $s=p+q$. Knowing $s$ is
equivalent to knowing the factorization, since $p$ and $q$ are the roots of
\[
  x^2-sx+N=0 .
\]
Suppose an external source --- a side channel, a partial leak, a structural constraint on
how $N$ was generated --- supplies an \emph{interval hint}: a promise that
$s\in[s_0-E,\;s_0+E]$, a window of $W=2E+1$ candidates. Brute force costs $W$
discriminant tests. We ask whether \emph{free} arithmetic structure can reduce that cost
below $W$ in a way that scales, i.e.\ whether it can \emph{amplify} the hint.

The most natural source of free structure is residue-local consistency. If $m$ is a small
prime not dividing $N$, then reducing $N=pq$ modulo $m$ constrains the residue of $s$:
with $\bar p\neq 0$ we have $\bar q=N\bar p^{-1}$, hence
\[
  \bar s=\bar p+N\bar p^{-1}\in T_m(N):=\{\,x+Nx^{-1}: x\in(\Zm{m})^{\times}\,\}.
\]
Candidates whose residue escapes $T_m(N)$ are discarded with certainty, and $T_m(N)$ is
tabulated in $O(m)$ operations once and consulted thereafter by table lookup. This is the
\emph{trace-set filter}: the least hidden invariant of the factorization, and hence the
strongest remaining candidate for a free amplifier.

\subsection{Results}
We settle the matter completely and negatively, with exact identities rather than
heuristics: exactness (\S\ref{sec:exact}), the fibre structure and discriminant
description (\S\ref{sec:fibres}), the exact size law (\S\ref{sec:size}), minimality
(\S\ref{sec:min}), emptiness of the factor filter (\S\ref{sec:pfilter}), the global
census (\S\ref{sec:census}), the no-amplification and isolation barriers including the
coupled case (\S\ref{sec:barrier}), the Fermat equivalence (\S\ref{sec:fermat}), and the
experimental confirmation with honest cost accounting (\S\ref{sec:exp}).

\section{Setting and definitions}

Throughout, $K$ denotes a finite field; the main case of interest is $K=\Zm{m}$ for a
prime $m$.

\begin{definition}[Trace set]
For $N\in K$, the \emph{trace set} of $N$ is
\[
  T(N)=\{\,x+N/x \;:\; x\in K,\ x\neq 0\,\}\subseteq K .
\]
When $K=\Zm{m}$ we write $T_m(N)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Square-root set]
$R(N)=\{x\in K: x^2=N\}$: the \emph{branch points} of the trace map $x\mapsto x+N/x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Exact filter]\label{def:exact}
A subset $S\subseteq K$ is an \emph{exact filter for $N$} if
$a\neq 0$ and $ab=N$ imply $a+b\in S$. Exactness is the minimal requirement on a filter
used inside a search: a non-exact filter may discard the true trace.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Survivors in a window]
Let $m_1,\dots,m_\omega$ be pairwise coprime moduli with filters $S_i\subseteq\Zm{m_i}$.
For integers $a$ and $W\geq 0$,
\[
  \Surv(a,W)=\{\,s\in\Z:\ a\le s<a+W,\ (s\bmod m_i)\in S_i\ \text{for all }i\,\}.
\]
When $S_i=T_{m_i}(N)$ we speak of \emph{trace survivors}.
\end{definition}

We write $\omega$ for the number of moduli, $M=\prod_i m_i$ for their product, and
$\chi_m$ for the Legendre symbol modulo an odd prime $m$.

\section{Exactness: zero false negatives}\label{sec:exact}

\begin{theorem}[Exactness]\label{thm:exactness}
Let $K$ be a field, $N\in K$, and suppose $ab=N$ with $a\neq 0$. Then $a+b\in T(N)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
From $ab=N$ and $a\neq 0$ we get $b=N/a$, hence $a+b=a+N/a$, the image of the nonzero
element $a$ under the trace map.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Semiprime form]\label{cor:semiprime}
Let $m$ be prime, $N=pq$ with $p,q\in\Z$, and $m\nmid p$. Then
$(p+q)\bmod m\in T_m(N\bmod m)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Reduce mod $m$: $\bar p\neq0$ in the field $\Zm{m}$ and $\bar p\bar q=\bar N$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:exactness}.
\end{proof}

Corollary~\ref{cor:semiprime} holds at every prime simultaneously, so the true trace
survives the conjunction of \emph{all} the filters unconditionally. This is what the
experiment of \S\ref{sec:exp} measured as a $400/400$ survival rate at every
$\omega\le 20$; it also shows the filter is a legitimate pruning device, never
sacrificing correctness.

\section{Fibres of the trace map and the discriminant description}\label{sec:fibres}

The pruning power of the filter is exactly the failure of injectivity of the trace map,
and that failure is described by a single symmetry.

\begin{theorem}[Conjugate fibres]\label{thm:fibres}
Let $K$ be a field, $N\in K$, and $x,y\in K$ nonzero. Then
\[
  x+\frac{N}{x}=y+\frac{N}{y}
  \qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad
  y=x \ \ \text{or}\ \ y=\frac{N}{x}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Clearing denominators, the equation is equivalent to $(x^2+N)y=x(y^2+N)$, which
rearranges to the factored identity $(y-x)(xy-N)=0$. In a field one factor vanishes:
either $y=x$ or $xy=N$, i.e.\ $y=N/x$. Conversely both give equal traces, the second
because $N/(N/x)=x$.
\end{proof}

Thus a fibre of the trace map is a \emph{conjugate pair} $\{x,N/x\}$ --- the same local
factorization written in the two possible orders --- degenerating to a single point
exactly at the branch points $x^2=N$.

\begin{theorem}[Local discriminant description]\label{thm:disc}
Let $K$ be a field with $\operatorname{char}K\neq2$ and $N\neq0$. For $t\in K$,
\[
  t\in T(N)\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad t^2-4N\ \text{is a square in }K .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) If $t=x+N/x$ with $x\neq0$, then $(x-N/x)^2=(x+N/x)^2-4N=t^2-4N$.
($\Leftarrow$) If $y^2=t^2-4N$, put $u=(t+y)/2$, $v=(t-y)/2$ (legitimate as $2\neq0$).
Then $u+v=t$ and $uv=(t^2-y^2)/4=N$. Since $N\neq0$ we have $u\neq0$ and $v=N/u$, so
$t=u+N/u\in T(N)$.
\end{proof}

\section{The exact size of the filter}\label{sec:size}

\begin{theorem}[Exact census over a finite field]\label{thm:census}
Let $K$ be a finite field and $N\in K$ with $N\neq0$. Then
\[
  2\,\card{T(N)}=\bigl(\card{K}-1\bigr)+\card{R(N)} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $S=K\setminus\{0\}$, so $\card{S}=\card{K}-1$, and note $R(N)\subseteq S$ since
$N\neq0$. The trace map $\varphi(x)=x+N/x$ sends both $S$ and $R(N)$ into $T(N)$, so
partitioning each into fibres over $T(N)$,
\[
  \card{S}=\sum_{t\in T(N)}\card{\varphi^{-1}(t)\cap S},
  \qquad
  \card{R(N)}=\sum_{t\in T(N)}\card{\varphi^{-1}(t)\cap R(N)} .
\]
It suffices to show that for each $t\in T(N)$ the two fibre cardinalities sum to $2$. Fix
$t\in T(N)$ and $x\neq0$ with $\varphi(x)=t$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:fibres} the fibre in
$S$ is $\{x,N/x\}$.
If $x^2=N$ then $N/x=x$, the fibre in $S$ is $\{x\}$ and the fibre in $R(N)$ is $\{x\}$:
sum $1+1=2$.
If $x^2\neq N$ then $N/x\neq x$, the fibre in $S$ has two elements, and the fibre in
$R(N)$ is empty (a square root $y$ in the fibre satisfies $y=x$, contradicting
$x^2\neq N$, or $y=N/x$, which with $y^2=N$ forces $y=x$ again): sum $2+0=2$.
Summing over $t\in T(N)$ gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[At most two roots]\label{lem:roots}
$\card{R(N)}\le2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $r^2=N$ and $x^2=N$ then $(x-r)(x+r)=0$, so $x\in\{r,-r\}$; if no root exists,
$R(N)=\varnothing$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Half-size bounds]\label{thm:half}
For $N\neq0$ in a finite field $K$,
\[
  \card{K}-1\ \le\ 2\,\card{T(N)}\ \le\ \card{K}+1,
  \qquad\text{hence}\qquad
  \card{T(N)}\ \ge\ \frac{\card{K}-1}{2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:census} and Lemma~\ref{lem:roots}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Legendre form]\label{thm:legendre}
Let $m$ be an odd prime and $N$ an integer with $m\nmid N$. Then
\[
  2\,\card{T_m(N)}=m+\chi_m(N),
\]
so a uniformly random wrong candidate survives the filter at $m$ with probability exactly
\[
  \frac{\card{T_m(N)}}{m}=\frac12\Bigl(1+\frac{\chi_m(N)}{m}\Bigr)=\frac12\pm\frac1{2m}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:census} (with $\card K=m$) with the standard count
$\card{R(N)}=\chi_m(N)+1$ for $m$ odd and $N\not\equiv0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The trace-set filter delivers \emph{exactly one bit} of pruning per prime, with a relative
correction of size $1/m$; it is never an $m^{-c}$ filter for $c>0$. For
$N=3233=53\cdot61$ one has $\card{T_{13}}=7=(13+1)/2$ (with $\chi_{13}(N)=+1$) and
$\card{T_{17}}=8=(17-1)/2$ (with $\chi_{17}(N)=-1$).
\end{remark}

\section{Minimality: the residue-local family is capped at one bit}\label{sec:min}

\begin{theorem}[The trace set is the minimal exact filter]\label{thm:minimal}
Let $S\subseteq K$ be an exact filter for $N$ in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:exact}.
Then $T(N)\subseteq S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $t=x+N/x\in T(N)$ with $x\neq0$. Then $a=x$, $b=N/x$ satisfy $a\neq0$ and $ab=N$, so
exactness gives $a+b=t\in S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One-bit cap]\label{thm:onebit}
Let $N\neq0$ in a finite field $K$ and let $S$ be any exact filter for $N$. Then
\[
  \card{S}\ \ge\ \card{T(N)}\ \ge\ \frac{\card{K}-1}{2}.
\]
In particular, over $\Zm{m}$ with $m\ge5$ prime, every exact filter retains at least two
residues, and a wrong candidate survives with probability at least $\tfrac12-\tfrac1{2m}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:minimal} and Theorem~\ref{thm:half}; for $m\ge5$, $(m-1)/2\ge2$.
\end{proof}

No consistency test depending only on $N\bmod m$ can prune a wrong candidate with
probability better than roughly $1/2$. The trace filter attains the bound; the content is
that nothing beats it.

\section{The factor-residue filter is empty}\label{sec:pfilter}

\begin{theorem}[Emptiness of the factor filter]\label{thm:pfilter}
Let $K$ be a field and $N\in K$ with $N\neq0$. Then
\[
  \{\,a\in K:\ \exists b\in K,\ ab=N\,\}=K\setminus\{0\}=K^{\times}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $ab=N\neq0$ then $a\neq0$; conversely for $a\neq0$ take $b=N/a$.
\end{proof}

The admissible-factor set is all of $K^\times$, so the factor filter re-tests only
coprimality with $m$ --- a condition every prime candidate $p'\neq m$ satisfies
automatically. The measured survival rate of coprime candidates was $1.0000$: a
candidate-level zero-block.

\section{The global census: independence, blindness, exact counts}\label{sec:census}

\begin{theorem}[Chinese remainder independence]\label{thm:crt}
Let $m_1,\dots,m_\omega$ be pairwise coprime with $M=\prod_i m_i$ and let
$S_i\subseteq\Zm{m_i}$. The set of $z\in\Zm{M}$ reducing into $S_i$ at every $i$ has
cardinality exactly $\prod_{i=1}^{\omega}\card{S_i}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The Chinese remainder isomorphism $\Zm{M}\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\prod_i\Zm{m_i}$ is a ring
isomorphism whose $i$-th component is reduction; the set in question is the preimage of
$\prod_i S_i$, and a bijection preserves cardinality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Positional blindness]\label{thm:blind}
Let $M\ge1$ and $S\subseteq\Zm{M}$. For every integer $a$,
\[
  \#\{\,s\in\Z:\ a\le s<a+M,\ (s\bmod M)\in S\,\}=\card{S}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Reduction $s\mapsto s\bmod M$ restricted to $M$ consecutive integers is a bijection onto
$\Zm{M}$: injective because two elements of a window of length $M$ congruent mod $M$
coincide, and surjective since $z$ is hit by $a+((z-a)\bmod M)$. Restrict the bijection
to the preimage of $S$.
\end{proof}

A residue filter therefore carries no positional information: it cannot say ``the trace
lies in the left half of the window'', only ``the trace lies in these residue classes'',
and every window meets the same number of them.

\begin{theorem}[Window census]\label{thm:window}
With pairwise coprime moduli $m_i>0$, filters $S_i$, and $M=\prod_i m_i$, every window of
$M$ consecutive candidates contains exactly $\prod_i\card{S_i}$ survivors, independently
of the window's location.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorems~\ref{thm:crt} and~\ref{thm:blind}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Linear scaling in width]\label{thm:linear}
For every $k\ge0$ and every $a$, the window $[a,\,a+kM)$ contains exactly
$k\prod_i\card{S_i}$ survivors.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Split into $k$ disjoint blocks of length $M$ and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:window} to each;
formally, induct on $k$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:linear} is the quantitative heart of the negative result: the survivor
count is exactly proportional to the width of the hint. The filter fixes a
\emph{density}, never a \emph{cardinality}, and density is precisely what cannot be traded
for resolution.

\begin{theorem}[Exact trace census]\label{thm:tracecensus}
Let $m_1,\dots,m_\omega$ be distinct odd primes with $m_i\nmid N$ and $M=\prod_i m_i$.
Every window of $M$ consecutive candidate traces contains exactly
\[
  \#\Surv=\frac{1}{2^{\omega}}\prod_{i=1}^{\omega}\bigl(m_i+\chi_{m_i}(N)\bigr)
\]
survivors; in particular
$\prod_i(m_i-1)\le 2^{\omega}\,\#\Surv\le\prod_i(m_i+1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:window} gives $\#\Surv=\prod_i\card{T_{m_i}(N)}$; multiply the local
identities of Theorem~\ref{thm:legendre} and bound $\chi\in\{\pm1\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
$N=3233=53\cdot61$ with moduli $\{3,5,7\}$ has $\card{T_3}=1$, $\card{T_5}=2$,
$\card{T_7}=3$, so $M=105$ and every window of $105$ consecutive candidates --- starting
at $0$, at $500$, at $99999$, anywhere --- contains exactly $1\cdot2\cdot3=6$ survivors,
one of which (in the appropriate window) is the true trace $114=53+61$.
\end{example}

\section{No amplification and the isolation barrier}\label{sec:barrier}

\begin{theorem}[No amplification]\label{thm:noamp}
Let $m_1,\dots,m_\omega$ be distinct primes with $m_i\ge5$ and $m_i\nmid N$, and let
$S_i$ be any exact filters (in particular the trace filters). Any window of width
$W\ge M=\prod_i m_i$ retains at least $2^{\omega}$ surviving candidates.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:onebit}, $\card{S_i}\ge(m_i-1)/2\ge2$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:window} a
sub-window of width exactly $M$ already contains $\prod_i\card{S_i}\ge2^{\omega}$
survivors, and enlarging the window only adds survivors.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The filter never isolates]\label{thm:neveriso}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:noamp} with $\omega\ge1$, if $N=pq$ with
$m_i\nmid p$ for all $i$ and the true trace $p+q$ lies in the window, then the survivor
set contains $p+q$ together with at least one spurious candidate $s\neq p+q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The true trace survives by Corollary~\ref{cor:semiprime}; the count is at least
$2^{\omega}\ge2$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:noamp}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Isolation requires the primorial]\label{thm:iso}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:noamp} with $\omega\ge1$, if the survivor count
in a window of width $W$ is at most $1$, then $W<\prod_{i=1}^{\omega}m_i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Contrapositive of Theorem~\ref{thm:noamp}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Superpolynomial sieve cost]\label{cor:cost}
To reduce a hint window of width $W=2^{k}$ to a single candidate using distinct prime
moduli one needs $\prod_i m_i>2^{k}$. Since the smallest product of $\omega$ distinct
primes is the primorial $p_\omega\#=e^{(1+o(1))\omega\log\omega}$, this forces
$\omega=\Omega(k/\log k)$ primes with largest modulus of size $\Omega(k)$, so
constructing and consulting the filters costs $2^{\Omega(k/\log k)}$ --- superpolynomial
in the bit length.
\end{corollary}

For the unhinted search, Theorem~\ref{thm:linear} gives exactly
$k\prod_i\card{T_{m_i}}\ge k\,2^{\omega}$ survivors over $k$ full periods. Starting from a
range of $2^{24}$ traces, the surviving population decays as
$2^{24}\to2^{19}\to2^{13.3}\to2^{7.4}$ at $\omega=0,6,12,18$: exponential decay of the
survivors, matched by exponential growth in the cost of the moduli needed to continue.

\subsection{Coupled filters do not help}

Perhaps a filter examining the \emph{joint} residue modulo $M$ can exploit correlations.
It cannot, and the reason is again exactness.

\begin{theorem}[Coupled minimality]\label{thm:coupled}
Let $m_1,\dots,m_\omega$ be pairwise coprime, $M=\prod_i m_i$, $N\in\Zm{M}$, and let
$S\subseteq\Zm{M}$ be \emph{exact modulo $M$}, i.e.\ $a+b\in S$ whenever $a$ is a unit of
$\Zm{M}$ and $ab=N$. Then
\[
  \card{S}\ \ge\ \prod_{i=1}^{\omega}\card{T_{m_i}(N\bmod m_i)} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $z\in\Zm{M}$ reduce into $T_{m_i}(N\bmod m_i)$ at every $i$; by
Theorem~\ref{thm:crt} it suffices to show $z\in S$. For each $i$ choose $x_i\neq0$ in
$\Zm{m_i}$ with $x_i+N_i/x_i=z_i$, where $N_i,z_i$ are the reductions of $N,z$. The tuple
$(x_i)_i$ is a unit of $\prod_i\Zm{m_i}$ since each coordinate is nonzero in a field;
transport through the Chinese remainder isomorphism gives a unit $a\in\Zm{M}$, and
likewise $b$ corresponding to $(N_i/x_i)_i$. Then $ab=N$ and $a+b=z$ coordinatewise, hence
globally, and exactness of $S$ gives $z\in S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No coupled amplification]\label{thm:coupledamp}
If additionally each $m_i\ge5$ is prime and $N$ reduces to a nonzero residue mod each
$m_i$, then any exact $S\subseteq\Zm{M}$ accepts at least $2^{\omega}$ candidates in any
window of width $W\ge M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each local trace set has at least two elements by Theorem~\ref{thm:onebit}, so
$\card{S}\ge2^{\omega}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:coupled}; apply positional blindness
(Theorem~\ref{thm:blind}) and monotonicity in $W$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The seal, strongest form]\label{thm:seal}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:coupledamp} with $\omega\ge1$: if any exact
residue filter modulo $M$ isolates at most one candidate in a window of width $W$, then
$W<M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Contrapositive of Theorem~\ref{thm:coupledamp}.
\end{proof}

The barrier is thus not an artifact of testing primes independently, nor of choosing the
trace as the invariant: \emph{any} filter guaranteed never to discard the true trace must
accept at least half the residues at each prime, hence at least $2^{\omega}$ candidates in
a primorial-wide window.

\section{The trace scan is Fermat's method in disguise}\label{sec:fermat}

\begin{theorem}[Fermat equivalence over $\Z$]\label{thm:fermatint}
For integers $N$ and $s$,
\[
  \exists\,a,b\in\Z:\ ab=N\ \text{and}\ a+b=s
  \qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad
  \exists\,d\in\Z:\ d^2=s^2-4N .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Take $d=a-b$; then $d^2=(a+b)^2-4ab=s^2-4N$.
($\Leftarrow$) Given $d^2=s^2-4N$ we have $(s-d)(s+d)=4N$. The two factors have the same
parity and cannot both be odd, since their product is divisible by the prime $2$; hence
both are even. Write $s-d=2c$; then $s+d=2(s-c)$, and $c(s-c)=N$ with $c+(s-c)=s$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Explicit recovery]\label{thm:recover}
If $d^2=s^2-4N$ then $\frac{s-d}{2}$ and $\frac{s+d}{2}$ are integers with
\[
  \frac{s-d}{2}\cdot\frac{s+d}{2}=N,
  \qquad
  \frac{s-d}{2}+\frac{s+d}{2}=s .
\]
\end{theorem}

Consequently ``is $s$ an admissible trace?'' \emph{is} the question ``is $s^2-4N$ a
perfect square?'', and the trace scan over an interval is Fermat's difference-of-squares
scan re-parameterized. The residue filters are then the classical remark that a perfect
square must be a quadratic residue modulo every prime --- exactly the observation
underlying Fermat sieves. Such sieves reduce constants; Theorems~\ref{thm:iso}
and~\ref{thm:seal} quantify that they cannot move the exponent, because exactness pins
their local pruning rate at $1/2$ per prime.

\section{Experiment and honest cost accounting}\label{sec:exp}

The theory was tested against a controlled experiment: $400$ semiprimes $N=pq$ with
$24$-bit prime factors ($48$-bit $N$), trace-set and factor-set filters at
$\omega=0,\dots,20$ small primes, and a rejection-ordered scan with explicit accounting of
both discriminant tests and membership tests.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Exactness.} The true trace survived $400/400$ at every $\omega\le20$ --- a
direct instance of Corollary~\ref{cor:semiprime}.
\item \textbf{Local pruning rate.} Wrong candidates survived at rate $0.1233$ for
$\omega=3$ (idealized $2^{-3}=0.125$) and $0.0151$ for $\omega=6$ (idealized
$2^{-6}=0.015625$). The deficits are exactly the accumulated Legendre corrections
$\prod_i(1+\chi_i/m_i)$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:tracecensus}.
\item \textbf{Factor filter.} Coprime survival was $1.0000$: the factor filter blocks
nothing, exactly as Theorem~\ref{thm:pfilter} requires.
\item \textbf{Interval hint.} With half-width $E=4000$ (window $W=8001$), the number of
candidates reaching the expensive discriminant test dropped from $8001$ at $\omega=0$ to
$121.5$, $2.9$ and $1.1$ at $\omega=6,12,18$ --- matching $(2E{+}1)2^{-\omega}+1$, the
``$+1$'' being the true trace guaranteed to survive.
\item \textbf{The accounting that matters.} Those savings were bought, not found.
Determining \emph{which} candidates deserve a discriminant test required $\approx1.9$
membership tests per candidate across the entire window: between $15{,}294$ and $15{,}550$
lookups in total, essentially independent of $\omega$. The full range is still iterated;
the reduction in expensive tests is exactly offset by the cheap tests spent identifying
them --- cost parity or worse, exactly as positional blindness
(Theorem~\ref{thm:blind}) predicts.
\item \textbf{Unhinted search.} Survivors over a $2^{24}$ range fell as
$2^{24}\to2^{19}\to2^{13.3}\to2^{7.4}$ for $\omega=0,6,12,18$, in agreement with
Theorem~\ref{thm:linear}. Driving the survivor count to a polynomial in the bit length $k$
would require a modulus product of size $e^{\Theta(k\log k)}$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Why the barrier is structural}
Every result above is an identity, not a bound with slack. Exactness forces a filter to
accept the trace of \emph{every} local factorization (Theorem~\ref{thm:minimal}); local
factorizations are parameterized by units $x\mapsto(x,N/x)$ and the trace map has fibres
of size exactly two (Theorem~\ref{thm:fibres}); hence every exact filter has at least
$\approx m/2$ elements --- one bit, exactly (Theorem~\ref{thm:onebit}); residue filters
are positionally blind and CRT-independent (Theorems~\ref{thm:crt}, \ref{thm:blind}), so
their effect on a window is exactly multiplicative and exactly proportional to the width
(Theorems~\ref{thm:window}, \ref{thm:linear}); therefore isolation requires the modulus
product to exceed the width (Theorems~\ref{thm:iso}, \ref{thm:seal}), at superpolynomial
cost (Corollary~\ref{cor:cost}). No step in this chain can be weakened by better filter
design; the only assumption is exactness.

\subsection{The free-information mirage}
The trace-set filter is a clean specimen of a recurring phenomenon: a test can be free
(tabulating $T_m(N)$ costs $O(m)$ once), sound (never rejects the truth) and sharp
(rejects half the impostors per prime), and still be useless for amplification, because
its selectivity is proportional to its periodicity. The rejection rate $2^{-\omega}$ and
the period $M=\prod_i m_i$ are locked together.

\subsection{What is not claimed}
We do not claim factoring is hard, nor that no hint-amplification algorithm exists. The
theorems bound one natural class: filters determined by residues modulo small moduli that
never reject the true trace. Algorithms using non-local information (lattice reduction on
the hint interval, in the spirit of Coppersmith-style partial-information attacks), or
accepting a probability of failure, fall outside the model, and are where the remaining
hope lies.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[The half-bit barrier is a character barrier]
Let $f\in\Z[X,Y]$ be absolutely irreducible and symmetric, and define the $f$-profile
filter $T_m^f(N)=\{f(a,b)\bmod m:\ ab\equiv N\bmod m,\ a\in(\Zm{m})^{\times}\}$. Then
$\card{T_m^f(N)}/m\to1/\deg_Y f$ as $m\to\infty$, with error $O(m^{-1/2})$ given by a Weil
bound. In particular no symmetric invariant of the factor pair prunes more than a
$1/\deg$ fraction, and the rate is never $m^{-c}$ for $c>0$.
\end{conjecture}

The identity $2\card{T}=m+\chi(N)$ is the degree-two case of counting points on the curve
$f(a,N/a)=t$: the density is governed by the number of $t$-fibres, i.e.\ by the degree of
the fibre map, not by any arithmetic secret of $N$.

\begin{conjecture}[Free filters cannot beat the primorial]
For every family of exact filters $S_m\subseteq\Zm{m}$ computable from $N\bmod m$ in
$\operatorname{poly}(\log m)$ time, and every hint window of width $W$, the survivor count
is at least $W/\prod_m m$ whenever $\prod_m m\le W$. Consequently isolating a $k$-bit hint
requires moduli of product $2^{\Omega(k)}$, hence sieve work $2^{\Omega(k/\log k)}$.
\end{conjecture}

Exactness forces $S_m\supseteq T_m$ and the Chinese remainder theorem makes the survivor
count exactly multiplicative; the coupled case is settled by Theorem~\ref{thm:coupled}, so
what remains is a finite complexity-theoretic accounting of sieve cost.

\begin{conjecture}[Hint amplification is a square-root oracle]
An algorithm that, given $N$ and a hint interval of width $W$, returns the true trace
using $\operatorname{poly}(\log N)$ filter evaluations is equivalent to an oracle producing
square roots modulo $N$, and hence to factoring itself.
\end{conjecture}

The Fermat equivalence of \S\ref{sec:fermat} is the reason to expect this: admissibility of
a trace \emph{is} squareness of the discriminant, so an amplifier is a device for locating
squares in an arithmetic progression.

Two further directions seem worthwhile: an exact analysis of non-exact (one-sided-error)
filters, quantifying the trade-off between false-negative probability and pruning rate;
and a formal cost model for rejection-ordered scans making the accounting of
\S\ref{sec:exp} a theorem --- the statement ``a positionally blind filter cannot reduce the
number of positions touched'' deserves proof in a machine model rather than measurement.

\section{Conclusion}

The trace-set filter is exact: the true trace survives every modulus, always. It is
exactly half-sized: $2\card{T_m(N)}=m+\chi_m(N)$, one bit of pruning per prime with a
$1/m$ correction. It is minimal: every filter that never rejects the truth contains it, so
no residue-local test does better, not even one coupling all primes at once. It is
positionally blind and CRT-multiplicative, so its census in a window is exactly
$2^{-\omega}\prod_i(m_i+\chi_i)$ times the width, wherever the window sits. And it is
therefore incapable of amplifying an interval hint: isolating a candidate requires the
product of the moduli to exceed the width of the hint, at superpolynomial cost, and the
discriminant tests it saves are traded one-for-one against the membership tests it spends.

The trace-set filter is an impeccable consistency certificate that conveys no usable
information. Its exactness is precisely what makes it useless: a test that can never be
wrong about the answer can never be very selective about the alternatives.

